Verify that two computers can speak to each other.
Used 'ping [IP address of computer B]' from command prompt, from computer A [host].
Got 'timed out', repeatedly.  
Destination PC is safely ignoring my ping because the Windows Firewall blocks inbound IPv6 echo requests by default.

To fix this, I need to tell the destination computer's firewall that it is allowed to respond to IPv6 pings.

In CP I ran:   
netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="Allow IPv6 Inbound Ping" dir=in action=allow protocol=icmpv6 profile=private
Response: Ok

Go to host computer to ping destination IPv6 address
I got reply from the destination computer.  

Now that the underlying network fabric is responsive, I am moving back up to my primary goal: establishing the local Windows SMB file share lab over IPv6.  

Verify and Enable the SMB Protocols  

Before creating a folder share, I must ensure that the Windows SMB server features and network protocols are enabled and listening on my network interfaces  

Run the following commands on both machines in an Administrative PowerShell window to lay the groundwork: (Run both commands in PowerShell Admin.)

1. Verify the SMB Server Configuration
Ensure your machines are configured to allow inbound SMB traffic:
powershell
Get-SmbServerConfiguration | Select-Object EnableSMB1Protocol, EnableSMB2Protocol  

2. Verify SMB Binding to IPv6
Ensure that the SMB server is actively listening on your IPv6 interfaces:
powershell
Get-SmbServerNetworkInterface

